---
// Figure: a command that timed out on the client still runs on the server.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import './clients.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="clients-timeout" title="A timeout does not cancel the command" controls="steps"
  caption="A sequence of steps, not a timed simulation. I ran all three cases on Redis 8.10.3 with redis-py 8.1.0's default retries: DEBUG SLEEP 0.5 on one connection held the main thread and the application's 100 ms timeout fired. INCR views went from 100 to 102; SET price 35 left 35; with writer B's SET price 36 sent 50 ms later, the price ended at 35 every time.">
  <Choice slot="params" name="command" label="The command" value="incr"
    options={[{ value: 'incr', label: 'INCR views' }, { value: 'set', label: 'SET price 35' }, { value: 'race', label: 'SET price 35, while B writes' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>Another client's slow command holds Redis's main thread. The application sends its command, which waits unread in the connection's socket buffer; after 100 ms the application gives up and closes the connection. When the slow command finishes, Redis still reads and runs the waiting command, and its reply goes nowhere. The application then retries.</p>
    <p>With INCR views, both the original and the retry run, so views goes from 100 to 102: one page view counted twice. With SET price 35, running it twice gives the same result as running it once: price is 35.</p>
    <p>With SET price 35 while writer B stores a newer price, 36: B's command waits behind the application's, so Redis runs SET 35 and then SET 36, and the price is right. Then the application's retry arrives and sets it back to 35. The application sees OK, and B's newer price is lost.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { switchSequence } from './switch-sequence.mjs';
  import { timeoutSpecs } from './sequences.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="clients-timeout"]').forEach(root => switchSequence(root, timeoutSpecs, 'command'));
</script>
